      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the fundamental principle of counting, which states that if there are \\( n \\) ways to do one thing and \\( m \\) ways to do another, then there are \\( n \\times m \\) ways to do both. We can apply this principle to each of the three categories (entrees, drinks, and desserts) to find the total number of distinct possible meals Crystal can buy.\n\n1. **Entrees:**\n   Crystal has 4 entrees to choose from:\n   - Pizza\n   - Chicken Teriyaki\n   - Corn Dog\n   - Fish and Chips\n\n2. **Drinks:**\n   Crystal has 2 drinks to choose from:\n   - Lemonade\n   - Root Beer\n\n3. **Desserts:**\n   Crystal has 2 desserts to choose from:\n   - Frozen Yogurt\n   - Chocolate Chip Cookie\n\nNow, we multiply the number of choices in each category to find the total number of distinct possible meals:\n\\[\n4 \\text{ (entrees)} \\times 2 \\text{ (drinks)} \\times 2 \\text{ (desserts)} = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of distinct possible meals Crystal can buy is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
